WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Imagine you're in a foreign country, and someone gives you directions in a language you barely understand.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
They rattle off five turns, two landmarks, and a distance. By the second instruction, you're lost.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
That's what multi-step instructions feel like for many of our pupils.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Chunking is beautifully simple: instead of giving three instructions at once, you give one. Wait. Confirm it's done. Then give the next.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Why does this work? It's about working memory. Some pupils can hold maybe one or two things in mind at once—not three or four.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:46.000
When we overload that capacity, everything falls apart. The pupil isn't being defiant or lazy; their mental inbox is simply full.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:56.000
EHCPs often describe this as difficulties following multi-step instructions or requires instructions broken down. That's your cue to chunk.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The beautiful thing? This strategy costs you nothing except a slight pause between instructions.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:14.000
And the payoff? A pupil who actually succeeds at the task, instead of sitting frozen or guessing what comes next.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
One instruction. One success. Then the next.
